WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:00.870
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Your application

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.730
is running on Google
Cloud, automatically

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.190
make some metrics available
through cloud monitoring.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:06.210
But there may be
times when you need

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.252
to add custom telemetry
to your system for better

00:00:08.252 --> 00:00:09.330
observability.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.700
Today, I'll show you how to use
OpenTelemetry to do just that.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.050
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.354
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.390
Previously, we covered
using OpenCensus for custom

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.710
metric instrumentation.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.910
That project is merging
with OPENTRACING,

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.390
and the next major release
is called OpenTelemetry.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.090
It's a single set of
APIs, libraries, agents,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.430
and collector services to
capture distributed traces

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.200
and metrics from
your application.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.462
Today, I'll show you how use
the metric functionality.

00:00:38.462 --> 00:00:40.170
Let's take a look at
a specific example--

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.960
a web server written in Node.js.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.303
We want to collect the golden
signals for our service--

00:00:44.303 --> 00:00:45.970
how much traffic we're
seeing, expressed

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.487
as a request count, how
many errors we're returning,

00:00:48.487 --> 00:00:50.570
and how quickly we're able
to respond to requests,

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.707
expressed as response latency.

00:00:52.707 --> 00:00:54.290
You can read more
about golden signals

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:55.832
in the Monitoring
Distributed Systems

00:00:55.832 --> 00:00:57.080
chapter of the SRE book.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.670
We'll link to that below.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:00:59.780
Let's get into the code.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.450
As always, we'll link to the
repo in the episode notes.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.192
For this example,
we'll send the metrics

00:01:04.192 --> 00:01:05.900
we'd like to Prometheus,
which we covered

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.430
in the previous episode.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.890
First, let's import
the necessary packages,

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:11.450
the opentelemetry/metrics
package

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.350
and the exporter-prometheus.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.920
Next, let's define the
metric we want to capture.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.330
Request_count and error_count
are monotonic counters,

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.380
meaning that we can only
increase their value.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.547
This makes sense, since the
total number of requests

00:01:23.547 --> 00:01:24.860
and errors only goes up.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.390
Latency is a
non-monotonic gauge,

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:28.930
which means it can
go up and down.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.930
Our latency will be
different for every request,

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.530
so we need to be able to
set its value directly.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.170
Finally, we're ready to
add values to our metrics.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.660
For our monotonic counters,
we can use the add() method

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.910
to increment them every time
we get a request or throw

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:41.780
an error.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.600
For the non-monotonic gauge,
we can use the set() method

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.120
to explicitly set the
value of the metric.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.890
We're now ready
to deploy the app,

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.515
making sure to properly add the
Prometheus scraping annotations

00:01:51.515 --> 00:01:52.820
to our deployment spec.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.490
Once that's done, we can
access the Prometheus UI

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:56.805
and graph our metrics.

00:01:56.805 --> 00:01:58.430
We can now see how
much traffic our app

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.550
is seeing, how reliable it is,
both in terms of availability

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:02.673
and performance.

00:02:02.673 --> 00:02:04.340
I hope you've enjoyed
learning about how

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.320
to use OpenTelemetry to
instrument your service

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.520
with custom metrics.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.898
I'm very excited about this
great open source project.

00:02:09.898 --> 00:02:11.690
It's still early, which
means that features

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.815
like a native Cloud Monitoring
exporter for metrics

00:02:13.815 --> 00:02:15.140
are still being developed.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.427
But that shouldn't stop
you from using it today.

00:02:17.427 --> 00:02:18.260
Thanks for watching.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.400
Come back next time.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.600
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:21.620
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

